WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  during my undergrad college years  for the most part i had a very good time  on campus  i made many strong friendships  but it just never really happened in my  dorm  my dorm was a place where i slept  not the place where i lived  my eagerness and enthusiasm to fit in  were met with mere  politeness there was a kind of  acknowledgement through small talk but  conversations never really progressed  i grew tired of walking into that dorm  feeling like such a stranger  i vividly recall walking through the  hallways of my dorm  on friday and saturday nights i would  hear  music playing a crowd of voices lots of  laughter  and the sound of parties behind closed  doors  at first i was surprised i'd never heard  about this parties happening  and with time i started to feel a bit  sad about it  of course i could have knocked on doors  or asked around but  who wants to invite themselves to a  party  i wanted to feel wanted  the experience taught me that an  invitation  is the highest form of compliment  i've thought about my undergrad dorm  experience for years  about the power of our mundane  interactions with one another  about the difference between being  polite and being present  about the difference between tolerance  and belonging  about the power and responsibility we  all share  to make wherever we live work and play  a more genuinely inclusive experience  for all of us  i've realized that invitations are  important but it's only part of it  for more clues i started to think of  times when i did feel a sense of  belonging in college and funny enough  every moment that came to mind had a  common denominator  my friend bright  yes his name is actually bright as in  bright light bright is from ghana  he immigrated to the us when he was 10  years old and developed a  strong interest in history maybe that's  why he also developed a genuine interest  in people which enables him to live up  to his name  bright is someone who has countless  times exemplified behaviors that create  belonging  after reflecting on how bright interacts  with people  i've learned four major lessons which i  want to share with you  with them i hope we can all make sure  that no one in our surroundings  ever feels the way i did in my undergrad  dorm  i call these lessons the right way  to foster belonging number one  as i've shared make invitations  they're the highest form of compliment  but there's more  number two make enthusiastic  introductions  bright never misses the opportunity to  introduce people to each other  and the most exciting way possible  i was once walking on campus and  inadvertently heard someone  scream my name hey jp  of course it was bright staining about  250 feet away from me  that's almost the length of a football  field  he was working with jerry and wanted to  make sure he introduced us to each other  when he finally reached me wright said  jp you have to meet jerry she's really  the coolest person here  he distilled and relayed the best parts  of her personalities  and accomplishments to each other he  made us feel  special he made us feel appreciated and  he made us feel  valued bright's glowing introduction  led to jerry and i building a strong  friendship still flourishing today  nine years later number three  make it a habit to reach out from those  who are closest to you  to those you don't see as often  for example bright's roommate was andy  andy was much more reserved than bright  but still  wright was always the first to remember  reaching out to andy  to check in on him and invite him to  things  i would hear him say things like hey  andy do you want to join us for dinner  andy i haven't seen you all day is  everything all right you wanna come and  study with us  he made a point to notice not only who  is present  but also who is absent understandably  we may sometimes hesitate on reaching  out to certain people for  fear of sounding too pushy  but bright's way is unique because for  him  the fear of a friend feeling excluded  always supersedes to fear of what others  might think of him  checking on those who may seem  extroverted as well  i remember a friend who i thought was  doing well as she was always surrounded  by people  after her second year she transferred  schools and it turns out she was not  doing so well  it turns out she did not feel a sense of  belonging in our community  i wish i'd reached out now i know  now you know  number four when you do reach out  really make time to connect  wright was affectionately called uncle  bright  by many of our friends because he made  time for them he made it clear he was  ready and willing to listen  he stopped what he was doing he made eye  contact  listen when you spoke did not interrupt  and asked questions  and he didn't do it with just people in  his close circle he did this with  everyone for example  i remember going to one of our dining  halls we're always greeted by john  on the kitchen staff john never failed  to shout bride's name hey bright so good  to see you  because bright will do the same hey john  how are you  so good to see you too  you see bright was not simply respectful  of those who supported us like  library staff dining hall workers and  maintenance crews in fact  that should be expected of everyone what  he did differently was  actually taking the time to get to know  people and have a genuine conversation  with everyone he came across  of course not everyone is as extroverted  as bright  it can also be toxic to always engage as  enthusiastically as he does especially  if he doesn't come second nature to us  the goal here is really to engage in  these behaviors as often as we can  without depleting ourselves for example  as an introvert  i found ways to adapt to lessons i've  learned from pride  in ways that i can digest  here's how i apply these principles and  of course i'm still working on doing  better  i consistently look for opportunities to  make invitations  and i try to make them personal and  intentional  for example instead of making a general  dinner invitation to a large group  i would prefer reaching out to each  person individually and let them know i  would love it  if they could be there the signal i hope  to send  is that i appreciate them and would love  to share my time  my most valuable asset with them  i do my best to check in on close  friends from time to time and put in an  extra effort to reach out to those i've  not heard from in a while  and when i do reach out to people  sometimes the interaction can last an  hour  or it could be just five minutes what  really matters  is to be present and intentional  so whether it has extroverted as bright  or more introverted like me remember  make invitations make enthusiastic  introductions  make it a habit to reach out from those  you're closest to to those you don't see  as often  and when you do reach out really make  time  to connect  and if you find yourself  secluded with no one  expressing belonging behaviors towards  you  i encourage you to seek to be the friend  you wish you had to others  you'll not only benefit but others will  be  grateful to you be brave  and be bright

